WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:03.400
Deciding on the perfect ending for a movie
can be tough.

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.450
Filmmakers have to end their story on just
the right note, or audiences might go home

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:11.539
angry — and sometimes, that can involve
a lot of trial and error.

00:00:11.539 --> 00:00:16.320
Case in point: These flicks, which underwent
dramatic changes in the endings department.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:21.320
Die Hard with a Vengeance finds John McClane
going toe-to-toe with Simon Gruber, a riddle-loving

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:23.410
terrorist with a grudge.

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:27.670
After dropping a bunch of bombs and brain-teasers,
McClane discovers that Gruber is actually

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:33.280
more interested in stealing $140 billion in
gold bullion than blowing anything up.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:38.270
So along with his reluctant sidekick, Zeus
Carver, McClane tracks Gruber down but finds

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:43.069
himself in a tricky situation when the bad
guy gets both a machine gun and a helicopter.

00:00:43.069 --> 00:00:47.269
Thinking on his feet, McClane shoots a powerline,
and the falling cables bring down Gruber's

00:00:47.269 --> 00:00:49.149
chopper...and that's it.

00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:53.350
For most fans of the franchise, it lacks the
satisfying personal touch that made the showdown

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:57.850
in the first Die Hard so memorable, but that's
not for lack of trying.

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:01.629
In the original ending, Gruber escapes to
Europe with all the gold, where he's living

00:01:01.629 --> 00:01:04.629
the easy life when an angry McClane shows
up.

00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:08.660
In this version, McClane has been kicked off
the force as the feds suspect him of helping

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:09.660
Gruber.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:11.660
But now, the ex-cop wants revenge.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:16.860
He puts a Chinese rocket launcher - one missing
the directional arrows —-on the table between

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:19.720
them, and he starts asking Gruber riddles.

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:23.500
The terrorist doesn't know which way the weapon
is aiming, and when he finally gets a riddle

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.200
wrong, he's forced to pull the trigger.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:26.600
"You sure?"

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:27.640
"Oh yes."

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:37.160
Unfortunately for Gruber, it's aimed straight
at his chest, and boom goes the terrorist.

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:41.700
It's a pretty epic ending, but according to
screenwriter Jonathan Hensleigh, the studio

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.830
thought it was, quote, "too cruel."

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:47.820
Admittedly, it would've been a dark move for
McClane, but it does make a somewhat more

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:49.640
satisfying showdown.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:53.710
Starring Michael Douglas and Glenn Close,
Fatal Attraction was a massive hit when it

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:55.210
was released in 1987.

00:01:55.210 --> 00:02:00.159
It was the second highest-grossing film that
year, picked up six Oscar nominations, and

00:02:00.159 --> 00:02:04.640
introduced audiences to Alex Forrest, the
seventh-best villain of all time according

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:06.500
to the American Film Institute.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:10.690
However, this thriller might not have been
so popular if the filmmakers had stuck with

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:11.980
the original ending.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:16.300
If you're not familiar with the plot, Fatal
Attraction is about a married man named Dan

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:20.220
Gallagher who has an affair with the aforementioned
Alex Forrest.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:24.390
When he tries to end the relationship, Alex
goes a little psycho and attempts to murder

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:25.920
Dan's wife.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:30.190
Before she can do the deed, Dan tries to drown
her in a bathtub, and then his wife finishes

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:31.480
Alex off.

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:35.730
It's a violent climax to a twisted thriller,
and audiences ate it up.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:39.530
However, the original finale was far kinder
to Ms. Forrest.

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:43.540
In this alternate ending, Dan is framed for
murder, but Alex eventually writes a letter

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:45.170
that frees him from blame.

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:46.900
"Alex Forrest is dead."

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:47.900
"I'm sorry.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:49.930
Your husband is under arrest."

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:51.640
Test audiences were appalled.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:55.819
They wanted blood, and even though Close and
director Adrian Lyne were opposed to changing

00:02:55.819 --> 00:03:00.530
the ending, Paramount executive Ned Tanen
ordered the filmmakers, quote, "to terminate

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:02.800
the b---- with extreme prejudice."

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:08.080
Released in 2017, Happy Death Day tells the
story of Tree, a college student caught in

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:10.130
a Groundhog Day-style time loop.

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:14.430
Of course, Tree's predicament is a lot worse
than being stuck in a sleepy Pennsylvania

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:16.410
town with Ned Ryerson.

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:20.900
She's being stalked by a masked killer, and
every time she dies, her day starts over and

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:23.810
she has to face the mysterious murderer yet
again.

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:29.349
Eventually, Tree realizes the killer is - spoiler
alert! - her sorority sister Lori, and after

00:03:29.349 --> 00:03:33.239
eating a poisoned cupcake, she defeats Lori
and breaks the loop.

00:03:33.239 --> 00:03:37.910
It's a satisfying ending - but the flick's
original finale wasn't quite as hopeful.

00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:41.990
In this alternate version, Tree is actually
murdered in the final few seconds by the wife

00:03:41.990 --> 00:03:44.330
of the professor she was having an affair
with.

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:48.160
The film implies she'll be stuck in the time
loop for a long, long time.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:52.960
"Did you really think you'd get away with
screwing my husband?"

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:55.200
"You gotta be kidding me."

00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:59.280
Test audiences hated it.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:01.739
Director Christopher Landon explained to CinemaBlend:

00:04:01.739 --> 00:04:05.730
"[Audiences] were like, 'Wait, she did all
this hard work, and made all these changes

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:07.349
and she's a better person now.

00:04:07.349 --> 00:04:09.099
And then you kill her again?'"

00:04:09.099 --> 00:04:14.569
On top of that, Landon originally intended
for there to be two killers: Lori and Gregory

00:04:14.569 --> 00:04:17.160
Butler, the professor Tree was messing around
with.

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:20.400
But at the end of the day, he went with just
one villain and made sure that Tree would

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:23.800
never get caught in a time loop again, until
part two, anyway.

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:27.160
"No way.

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:28.600
I don't believe this.

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:30.560
It's Monday the 18th… again!

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:31.720
AHHHHHHHH!!!"

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:35.400
When it comes to movies with dramatically
changed endings, the most infamous of the

00:04:35.401 --> 00:04:38.710
bunch has got to belong to Little Shop of
Horrors.

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:43.350
Directed by Frank Oz, this musical finds Rick
Moranis playing Seymour, a geeky florist in

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:46.130
love with his blonde bombshell of a coworker,
Audrey.

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:51.960
In his quest to impress her, Seymour begins
raising a venus fly trap he names Audrey II,

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:55.720
but as Seymour quickly discovers, this is
no normal plant.

00:04:55.720 --> 00:05:00.320
Not only can this thing talk, it also feeds
on human blood, and it gets bigger with every

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:01.320
meal.

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:06.130
A couple of corpses later, Audrey II is gigantic
and plans on devouring Seymour and his lady

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:07.130
love.

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:12.440
And this is where the original and theatrical
endings diverge in wildly different directions.

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:17.440
In the original version, Audrey II takes sadistic
glee in devouring our two heroes.

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:21.750
Even worse, a whole bunch of flesh-hungry
flowers start spreading across the world.

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:27.090
They grow to gigantic sizes, chase frightened
citizens, and destroy every building in sight.

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:31.419
It's an incredibly impressive sequence with
some truly staggering puppet work.

00:05:31.419 --> 00:05:36.470
According to The Dissolve, the climax cost
$5 million of the film's $25 million budget.

00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:41.419
But when Little Shop was screened for test
audiences, as Oz put it, quote, "The theater

00:05:41.419 --> 00:05:44.100
became a refrigerator, an ice box."

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:49.199
Audiences went icy cold the moment Audrey
II ate Seymour and his hapless girlfriend.

00:05:49.199 --> 00:05:54.170
So the filmmakers scrapped the plant-pocalypse
and let the two heroes live happily ever after.

00:05:54.170 --> 00:05:56.760
That is, if they manage to ignore this little
guy right here.

00:05:57.160 --> 00:06:00.950
The Evil Dead franchise isn't known for its
upbeat endings.

00:06:00.950 --> 00:06:05.419
The first movie finds Ash Williams dragged
off by a demon, and Evil Dead II sends our

00:06:05.419 --> 00:06:07.930
screaming hero back to the Middle Ages.

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:11.270
Army of Darkness, on the other hand, ends
on a much happier note.

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:16.419
After defeating an army of spirits and skeletons,
Ash escapes the Dark Ages by drinking a potion

00:06:16.419 --> 00:06:18.550
and saying some magic words.

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:22.700
He winds up back in the modern day, working
at a department store, and killing one last

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:24.760
Deadite that hitched a ride into the future.

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:30.510
[gunshots]

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:36.760
However, this wasn't the ending that director
Sam Raimi intended.

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:41.630
Originally, Ash was supposed to drink six
drops of a magic potion - one for each century

00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:44.169
- and then he'd fall into a long slumber.

00:06:44.169 --> 00:06:46.510
When he woke up, he'd be back in the present.

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:52.039
However, as he's counting out the drops, Ash
gets distracted, loses count, drinks too much,

00:06:52.039 --> 00:06:54.699
and wakes up in a post-apocalyptic future.

00:06:54.699 --> 00:06:58.360
The world has been reduced to rubble, and
it's too much for Ash to take.

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:00.110
"No!

00:07:00.110 --> 00:07:01.110
NO!

00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:03.910
I slept too long!"

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:08.740
Universal Pictures wasn't crazy about this
grim ending, so Raimi gave us the epic S-Mart

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:09.780
battle instead.

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:14.260
Granted, the alternate finale is more in line
with the nihilistic tone of the series, but

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:18.720
watching Ash go full John Wayne on a Deadite
is still pretty awesome, so we're not complaining

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:20.800
about studio interference this time.

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:26.080
Directed by Edgar Wright, Scott Pilgrim vs.
the World is an action-packed romantic adventure

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:31.030
where, basically, Michael Cera disses Captain
Marvel, gets in a bass battle with Superman,

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:36.199
and exchanges blows with Captain America,
all in a quest to date the girl of his dreams,

00:07:36.199 --> 00:07:37.570
Ramona Flowers.

00:07:37.570 --> 00:07:42.220
Playing the titular Scott Pilgrim, Cera has
to defeat Ramona's seven evil exes if he wants

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:43.590
to win her hand.

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:47.910
One by one, he beats them into oblivion and
finally gets to date the rainbow-haired object

00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:49.259
of his affection.

00:07:49.259 --> 00:07:54.250
Sadly, that means he leaves his ex-girlfriend,
Knives Chau, but she seems okay with Scott's

00:07:54.250 --> 00:07:55.250
choice.

00:07:55.250 --> 00:07:58.800
"I'm too cool for you anyway.”

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:01.160
"Ciao, Knives."

00:08:01.160 --> 00:08:02.720
"Go."

00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:06.729
In the original ending, however, things took
a very different turn after Scott beat Ramona's

00:08:06.729 --> 00:08:11.699
final boyfriend Gideon, and the film culminated
in Ramona walking off into the distance as

00:08:11.699 --> 00:08:14.170
Scott and Knives rekindled their romance.

00:08:14.170 --> 00:08:17.230
"Well, bye and stuff."

00:08:17.230 --> 00:08:19.530
"Yeah… and stuff."

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:23.850
Evidently, this was so controversial that
people at test screenings actually started

00:08:23.850 --> 00:08:28.990
arguing about it, so the filmmakers used their
1-up to start over and film a new ending.

00:08:28.990 --> 00:08:33.000
A third possible ending would've made fans
lose their minds.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:37.310
Wright briefly considered a last-minute twist
where we learn everything that had just happened

00:08:37.310 --> 00:08:42.250
was part of Scott's deranged fantasy and that
the slacker was actually a serial killer who'd

00:08:42.250 --> 00:08:44.910
just murdered seven innocent people.

00:08:44.910 --> 00:08:48.890
Whether you think Scott should've wound up
with Knives or Ramona, we can probably all

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:52.000
agree it was wise for Wright to skip this
crazy ending.

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:56.400
Love it or hate it, the ending of The Force
Awakens is definitely one of the most memorable

00:08:56.420 --> 00:08:58.100
in the Star Wars franchise.

00:08:58.100 --> 00:09:03.190
After hearing so much about Luke Skywalker,
Rey finally finds the Jedi master on the island

00:09:03.190 --> 00:09:04.560
of Ahch-To.

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:09.190
As the John Williams music starts to swell,
Rey offers the bearded knight his long-lost

00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:11.680
lightsaber, and then we cut to credits.

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:15.589
It's a classic ending, re-introducing audiences
to a beloved hero.

00:09:15.589 --> 00:09:20.320
However, The Last Jedi quickly - and, pretty
controversially —-subverts that ending when

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:23.800
Luke grabs the lightsaber and tosses it into
the ocean.

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:27.730
After making a grave mistake involving his
nephew, Kylo Ren, Luke has been trying to

00:09:27.730 --> 00:09:31.920
cut himself off from the Force, hoping the
Jedi Order will die with him.

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:36.100
Interestingly, this character arc ended up
changing the original ending of The Force

00:09:36.100 --> 00:09:40.520
Awakens, as J.J. Abrams had initially intended
to end his film with the Jedi Knight showing

00:09:40.520 --> 00:09:42.970
off his otherworldly powers.

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:46.610
According to Mark Hamill, The Force Awakens
was supposed to end with Rey discovering Luke

00:09:46.610 --> 00:09:51.120
on Ahch-To, where he would be surrounded by
floating boulders - no doubt a cool nod to

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:52.830
The Empire Strikes Back.

00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:57.510
However, before The Force Awakens hit theaters,
Hamill was reading The Last Jedi script and

00:09:57.510 --> 00:10:02.140
realized The Force Awakens' ending didn't
match up with Jedi's new direction.

00:10:02.140 --> 00:10:04.790
So he called up the filmmakers to see what
was happening.

00:10:04.790 --> 00:10:09.290
Fortunately, the folks at Lucasfilm had picked
up on the problem and altered the ending before

00:10:09.290 --> 00:10:10.280
the film hit theaters.

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:16.040
Loki's death in Avengers: Infinity War sparked
a lot of controversy among Marvel fans, but

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:19.330
this wasn't the first time the god of mischief
had met his maker.

00:10:19.330 --> 00:10:23.190
In the original cut of Thor: The Dark World,
Loki is actually killed in his battle with

00:10:23.190 --> 00:10:26.500
Algrim, the monstrous elf with a bad attitude.

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:31.029
So when Thor and Loki say their heartbreaking
goodbyes - the one so hysterically parodied

00:10:31.029 --> 00:10:32.029
in Thor: Ragnarok.

00:10:32.029 --> 00:10:34.530
"I'm sorry I tried to rule Earth."

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:36.490
"Ah, they'd be lucky to have you."

00:10:36.490 --> 00:10:39.350
"I'm sorry about that… thing with the Tesseract.

00:10:39.350 --> 00:10:40.940
I just couldn't help myself!"

00:10:40.940 --> 00:10:43.209
That was supposed to be for real.

00:10:43.209 --> 00:10:47.750
However, Loki fans weren't going to stand
idly by and let the MCU get away with murder.

00:10:47.750 --> 00:10:50.640
As Tom Hiddleston explained on the Empire
Film Podcast:

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:53.649
"In test screenings, the audience didn't accept
it.

00:10:53.649 --> 00:10:57.300
There was a very strange and almost unanimous
resistance to it."

00:10:57.300 --> 00:11:01.550
So Marvel was forced to change the ending
of the film, revealing that Loki was alive

00:11:01.550 --> 00:11:04.330
and well and looking a lot like Anthony Hopkins.

00:11:04.330 --> 00:11:08.769
While it might've felt like a cop-out at the
time, it wonderfully set up everything that

00:11:08.769 --> 00:11:13.600
happened in Ragnarok, so this is one time
where test audiences did a marvel-ous job.

00:11:13.640 --> 00:11:19.910
Directed by Alexander Payne, 1999's Election
is a black comedy with an infamously cynical

00:11:19.910 --> 00:11:20.910
ending.

00:11:20.910 --> 00:11:25.200
Matthew Broderick plays Jim McAllister, a
high school teacher who absolutely hates one

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:30.089
of his students: the overeager and narcissistic
Tracy Flick, played by Reese Witherspoon.

00:11:30.089 --> 00:11:35.090
Of course, McAllister isn't exactly the most
mature dude in the room, as he's a grown man

00:11:35.090 --> 00:11:38.580
trying to sabotage the ambitions of a high
school student.

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:42.540
So when he's caught rigging the election for
student body president against Tracy, he's

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:44.870
promptly fired from his job.

00:11:44.870 --> 00:11:49.240
In the theatrical ending, McAllister moves
to New York, becomes a museum tour guide,

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:52.260
and then does a little sightseeing in Washington
D.C.

00:11:52.260 --> 00:11:56.580
That's when he spies Tracy working alongside
some prominent politician.

00:11:56.580 --> 00:12:01.240
Infuriated at her success, he throws a soft
drink at her car before running in the other

00:12:01.240 --> 00:12:02.240
direction.

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:06.080
However, there was actually a far sweeter
ending - which, if you'll believe it, was

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:11.720
discovered on an early VHS workprint purchased
at a flea market in 2011.

00:12:11.720 --> 00:12:16.620
In this original ending, McAllister and Tracy
actually let bygones be bygones.

00:12:16.620 --> 00:12:21.510
Pardon the quality - like we mentioned: this
clip was discovered on a VHS from the '90s.

00:12:21.510 --> 00:12:29.589
"Y'know, I was having some personal problems
last year, and I guess I took it out on you."

00:12:29.589 --> 00:12:32.850
In this alternate ending, McAllister never
moves to New York.

00:12:32.850 --> 00:12:37.100
Instead, he's still in his hometown, working
as a used car salesman.

00:12:37.100 --> 00:12:40.530
Tracy shows up, looking for a vehicle, and
the two start bonding.

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:44.790
McAllister apologizes for his bad behavior
and even consoles her when she admits she's

00:12:44.790 --> 00:12:47.050
worried about going to college.

00:12:47.050 --> 00:12:51.360
Tracy then asks McAllister to sign her yearbook,
and the film ends as the ex-teacher starts

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:53.180
writing, "Dear Tracy."

00:12:53.180 --> 00:12:57.980
Needless to say, this didn't go over well
with test audiences, as the sweet ending didn't

00:12:57.980 --> 00:13:00.150
mesh with such a satirical film.

00:13:00.150 --> 00:13:02.540
This is one vote that went the right way.

